  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a proposed model for how ACPA antibodies may arise and influence progression toward rheumatoid arthritis. On the left, a “dynamic citrulline response” is shown as a circular process around a cell-like structure coated with antibodies and immune complexes. Environmental triggers such as smoking, mucosal exposures, and possibly infections appear to feed into this response. Beneath that, the image highlights key ACPA features that may vary, including multireactivity, fine specificity, Fab glycosylation, and antibody subclass.\n\nThe right side links different functional ACPA profiles to clinical stages. Blue antibodies labeled anti-inflammatory point toward an asymptomatic state, while gray neutral antibodies sit between these extremes. Red pro-inflammatory antibodies are associated with progression toward erosive rheumatoid arthritis, with arrows suggesting they may also contribute earlier during an “at-risk arthralgia” phase. A vertical pathway runs from asymptomatic to at-risk arthralgia and then to erosive RA, emphasizing a disease continuum.\n\nOverall, the figure argues that not all ACPAs are equivalent: their biological properties may help determine whether a person remains symptom-free or progresses to inflammatory, erosive RA, and may therefore help identify risk and guide tailored therapy.",
